From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 19:43:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 10/15] kboot: hand the preloaded ANE firmware over on the
 T8132

iBoot preloads the Neural Engine firmware on the M4 (T8132) as it does on
the A18 Pro: the J604 boot ADT /arm-io/ane is "pre-loaded" with a __TEXT
and a __DATA segment range. aura's generic hand-over (ane_compatibles[],
dt_set_ane_node()) already covers the M4 Pro, M5 Pro and A18 Pro engines,
but not the M4's, so a T8132 ANE node never gets its firmware regions nor
/chosen/apple,ane-cold-boot, and the kernel driver cannot start the
preloaded firmware.

Add "apple,t8132-ane" to the list. The ADT lookup already falls back from
/arm-io/ane0 to /arm-io/ane, which is where the M4 has it.

On the J604 boot ADT the segments are TEXT 0xc0000 bytes at 0x10000ac8000
(remap 0x10000000000) and DATA 0x2ac000 bytes at 0x10001a2c000 (remap
0x100000c0000), both below the OS. tests/python/test_ane_preload.py gains
a J604 case with those values; without this change it fails at the
cold-boot marker.

Assisted-by: LLM
---
 src/kboot.c                      |  5 +++--
 tests/python/test_ane_preload.py | 10 ++++++----
 2 files changed, 9 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/kboot.c b/src/kboot.c
index 598b471c..9f8b758d 100644
--- a/src/kboot.c
+++ b/src/kboot.c
@@ -4656,8 +4656,8 @@ static int dt_set_ave(void)
 }
 
 /*
- * The M4 Pro, M5 Pro and A18 Pro Neural Engines run firmware that iBoot preloads into
- * DRAM below the OS: /arm-io/ane0 (M4 Pro, M5 Pro) or /arm-io/ane (A18 Pro) is
+ * The M4, M4 Pro, M5 Pro and A18 Pro Neural Engines run firmware that iBoot preloads into
+ * DRAM below the OS: /arm-io/ane0 (M4 Pro, M5 Pro) or /arm-io/ane (M4, A18 Pro) is
  * pre-loaded, with exactly a __TEXT and a __DATA segment range. Reserve both segments no-map and
  * give them to the ANE node as its "firmware-text" and "firmware-data" memory regions, with the
  * device addresses the firmware runs them at (their remap addresses) as apple,firmware-iovas. A DT
@@ -4674,6 +4674,7 @@ static int dt_set_ave(void)
 static const char *const ane_compatibles[] = {
     "apple,t6041-ane",
     "apple,t6050-ane",
+    "apple,t8132-ane",
     "apple,t8140-ane",
 };
 
diff --git a/tests/python/test_ane_preload.py b/tests/python/test_ane_preload.py
index 2847d84b..33a30551 100644
--- a/tests/python/test_ane_preload.py
+++ b/tests/python/test_ane_preload.py
@@ -1,11 +1,11 @@
 # SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
-"""The M4 Pro, M5 Pro and A18 Pro ANE preload handoff: reservations, memory regions and firmware
-IOVAs.
+"""The M4, M4 Pro, M5 Pro and A18 Pro ANE preload handoff: reservations, memory regions and
+firmware IOVAs.
 
 Runs kboot.c's dt_set_ane_firmware(), dt_set_ane_node(), dt_ane_fill_region() and
 dt_get_or_add_reserved_mem() as they are, on real FDTs built with aura's libfdt, against a
-fake ADT. Segment values are the J616s 26.6.2 /arm-io/ane0, the Neo (J700) 26.6.2 /arm-io/ane
-and the J714s 27.0 /arm-io/ane0 segment-ranges. The J714s DT also has a soc@200000000 ahead of
+fake ADT. Segment values are the J616s 26.6.2 /arm-io/ane0, the Neo (J700) 26.6.2 /arm-io/ane,
+the J714s 27.0 /arm-io/ane0 and the J604 (M4, 25G83 boot ADT) /arm-io/ane segment-ranges. The J714s DT also has a soc@200000000 ahead of
 the /soc that holds the ANE, which libfdt resolves "/soc" to.
 """
 
@@ -18,6 +18,8 @@ ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]
 
 
 BOARDS = {
+    "j604": dict(compat="apple,t8132-ane", node="ane@500000000", adt="/arm-io/ane",
+                 text=0x10000AC8000, data=0x10001A2C000, phys_base=0x10002000000, sibling=0),
     "j616s": dict(compat="apple,t6041-ane", node="ane@484000000", adt="/arm-io/ane0",
                   text=0x100009D8000, data=0x1000196C000, phys_base=0x10002000000, sibling=0),
     "j700": dict(compat="apple,t8140-ane", node="ane@400000000", adt="/arm-io/ane",
